    "body": "> You can use a custom module to reposition the iTerm prompt marker, but you'll likely also need a custom top-level `format` containing `${custom.iterm_prompt_marker}`\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, this workaround (just like the ones I came up with myself) just messes the prompt in a similar way.\r\n\r\nWith the thing in my `$format` I get the cursor `\\r`ing to the beginning upon any input that gets picked up by `zsh-auto-suggestions`: \r\n\r\n<img width=\"228\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6256096/167924949-cb5b2565-6ccf-4e28-a485-03a5912a3332.png\">\r\n\r\nThis doesn't happen without the mark injection or with its injection to other prompts.\r\n\r\nThank you for the explanation though, great to see there's hope with that PR! Until then I'm going back to have it interpreting the `add_newline` as the prompt mark.",
